   "schema_template": "saas_billing",
   "narrative_hooks": [
    "Typical customers are mid-market finance, HR or operations teams buying 20-500 seats on 12-month contracts with Net-30 invoices; SMB self-serve pays by card monthly.",
    "Q4 is the heaviest booking quarter (budget flush); Q1 renewals cluster on Jan 1 and drive a visible deferred-revenue step-up and DSO spike.",
    "ASC 606 governs revenue: subscription recognised ratably, implementation fees deferred over the expected customer life, multi-element deals require stand-alone selling price allocation.",
    "Commissions are capitalised (ASC 340-40) and amortised over 3-5 years, so sales headcount changes hit the balance sheet before the P&L.",
    "Common failure modes: involuntary churn from expired cards (20-30% of churn), discount leakage at renewal, over-provisioned usage never billed, migrating a billing system and double-counting invoices.",
    "Net revenue retention above 100% hides gross churn; boards ask for GRR, NRR and logo churn separately by cohort and ACV band.",
    "SOC 2 Type II and GDPR data-processing addenda gate enterprise deals; a failed audit can freeze a quarter's enterprise pipeline.",
    "Usage events arrive via Kafka/webhooks and are metered nightly; late-arriving events force restatements of usage-based invoices.",
    "The CFO watches CAC payback, burn multiple and rule of 40; the CRO watches pipeline coverage (3x) and win rate; the VP Eng watches replica lag and queue depth on invoice runs.",
    "Annual prepay (typically 17% discount) front-loads cash and makes operating cash flow look stronger than earnings in growth years."
   ]
