   "narrative_hooks": [
    "The plant runs three work-centre families (machining, welding/assembly, paint/finish); one of them is the constraint and every improvement elsewhere just builds WIP in front of it.",
    "Overhead is allocated on direct labour hours, so low-volume complex parts look profitable while high-volume simple parts subsidise them (classic ABC distortion).",
    "Standard costs are set annually; purchase price, labour rate, efficiency and volume variances are reported monthly and often mis-attributed.",
    "Raw steel and aluminium are bought on 8-12 week lead times with surcharges indexed to commodity prices; a 15% metals spike hits COGS two quarters later.",
    "Customers (OEMs) demand 98% on-time delivery and issue chargebacks for line-down events; big-box customers score OTIF.",
    "Common failure modes: phantom inventory from unrecorded scrap, cycle-count variances booked straight to COGS, work orders closed before completion to hit monthly shipments, setup-heavy schedules that starve the bottleneck.",
    "ISO 9001 and customer PPAP audits; OSHA recordables; EPA permits for paint lines are the regulatory touchpoints.",
    "The ERP (SAP/NetSuite-style) holds BOMs, routings and standard costs; the MES logs production and scrap per shift; the two disagree by a few percent every month.",
    "Seasonality follows OEM build schedules: Q3 shutdowns, Q4 year-end push, Q1 slow restart.",
    "The plant manager watches OEE and schedule adherence; the controller watches absorption variance and inventory days; the CEO watches backlog and book-to-bill."
