   "narrative_hooks": [
    "Brokerage margin is the spread between what the shipper pays and what the carrier is paid; in a falling spot market margins expand, in a rising one contracts go underwater.",
    "Carriers are paid in 15-30 days but shippers pay in 45-60, so growth consumes cash even when profitable.",
    "Peak season (Sept-Dec) and produce season (spring) drive capacity crunches; Chinese New Year creates an ocean import gap in Feb-Mar.",
    "FMCSA hours-of-service, hazmat endorsements, customs brokerage licences (CBP) and C-TPAT are the regulatory touchpoints.",
    "Common failure modes: duplicate carrier invoices paid twice, accessorial charges (detention, liftgate) billed by carriers but never re-billed to shippers, tracking events in local time mixed with UTC, claims for damage that age unresolved.",
    "A distribution-centre customer is scored on OTIF by Walmart/Target; the 3PL inherits chargebacks when picks are late or short.",
    "The TMS holds shipments, lanes and rate cards; the WMS holds inventory and picks; the ERP holds invoices; reconciliation across the three is monthly and manual.",
    "Bullwhip shows up as wholesale order variance 1.5-2x retail sales variance, so warehouse labour swings between overtime and idle.",
    "Fuel surcharges are indexed weekly to the DOE diesel average and passed through with lag.",
    "The COO watches load count, margin per load and tender rejection; the CFO watches DSO/DPO gap and the revolver; the warehouse GM watches lines per hour and dock-to-stock."
   ]
